  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "K \\text{ compact} \\implies \\text{every net } (x_\\alpha) \\text{ in } K \\text{ has a cluster point in } K,",
    "plain": "The standard definition of compactness is \"every open cover has a finite subcover.\" Dualizing gives the finite-intersection property: any family of closed sets with the finite-intersection property has nonempty total intersection. The goal is to use *this* form to prove the cluster-point characterization: every net (or filter) on a compact set clusters somewhere. The tails of a net form a descending family of closed sets with the FIP, so their intersection — the cluster points — is nonempty.",
